## AI Code Review

## Bugs and Logic Errors

**`log.info` no longer fail-soft after hello send**

In the original `.then()/.catch()` chain, an exception thrown by `log.info(...)` would have been silently swallowed by `.catch()`. The refactor intentionally moves the success log outside the `try` so it isn't misattributed as a send failure — the comment is correct about that motivation — but as a side effect, a thrown exception from `log.info` now propagates unhandled into the stream handler. Logging frameworks rarely throw, but the behavioral change is real. If the intent is fail-soft end-to-end, wrap the `if (helloSent)` block:

```ts
if (helloSent) {
  try {
    log.info("sync: hello sent", { ... });
  } catch {
    // log throw must not propagate into handler
  }
}
```

If the intent is to let log failures surface (e.g., for crash-loop detection), the current code is fine but the comment should say so explicitly.

**Execution continues after failed hello send**

After a failed send (`helloSent === false`), the handler falls through to the `if (cursorGap)` block and potentially sends more messages on what may be a dead socket. This was also true in the original code, so it's not a regression from this diff, but the comment "the dead socket self-cleans via the heartbeat path" implies the handler will keep trying to send until the heartbeat detects the dead connection — any subsequent sends will also fail silently. Consider an early `return` after a failed hello to avoid log noise from downstream sends.

## Security

The auth detail change (`"no auth context in stream handler"` → `"authentication unavailable"`) reduces information disclosure to the client. Positive change, no issues.

## Spec Compliance

The diagnostic rationale is attributed to spec 183 in comments on both the original and changed code. No `Feature:` annotations are added or modified in this diff, so there is nothing to cross-check against `code_aliases` in `specs/183-*/spec.md` — but worth confirming the spec's acceptance criteria explicitly cover the "success log outside try" constraint if this was a spec-driven fix.

## Performance

No concerns. The `try/catch` + boolean flag is semantically equivalent to the promise chain in throughput terms.

